This directory contains the original artwork for WS, WS-MAP and EME65 and
the WS branding kit. The CMake build does not generate the bitmaps, because
of the extra tools needed; they are committed, and regenerated by hand with
the scripts here whenever the artwork changes.

Authorship, fonts (exact names, versions, licences, SHA-256), colours,
geometry and notes for copyright or trademark filings are recorded in
ARTWORK-RECORD.md. Read it before changing or reusing the artwork.

Master artwork (SVG, letters are outlines, no font needed):

ws_icon.svg     - WS program icon, 1024x1024.
wsmap_icon.svg  - WS-MAP program icon, 1024x1024.
eme65_icon.svg  - EME65 program icon, 1024x1024.
ws_globe.svg    - the globe without letters, for the utilities' icon set.
installer_logo.svg - the 150x57 NSIS installer header (white background).
DragNDrop Background.svg - the macOS disk-image background.

branding/       - web logos and favicon set, banners and social preview,
                  A4 letterhead (SVG, PDF, 300 dpi header and footer strips).

Regenerating:

make_icons.py   - renders every committed bitmap from the SVGs: the three
    Windows .ico files (icons/windows-icons/wsjtx.ico, qmap/wsjt.ico,
    map65/wsjt.ico), the macOS iconsets, icons/Unix/ws_icon.png, the
    installer BMP, the disk-image background and the branding bitmaps.
    Needs rsvg-convert (librsvg) and Python 3 with Pillow:
        RSVG_CONVERT=/path/to/rsvg-convert python3 artwork/make_icons.py .
    The macOS .icns is still built from the iconset by iconutil on a Mac
    (see CMakeLists.txt).

design/         - the Python scripts that write the SVGs above
    (make_artwork.py, wsart.py, ttf2path.py, font_info.py). They need the
    DejaVu Sans 2.37 font files listed in ARTWORK-RECORD.md in a directory
    named fonts/ next to design/; those files are not committed.

make_graphics.sh - the former Inkscape + ImageMagick script for the old
    WSJT-X globe artwork, kept for reference. It no longer matches the
    files here; use make_icons.py.
